  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1046 PM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nA developing omega block will keep high pressure locked in place for \nthe foreseeable future. The shortwave trough that pushed through \nthis evening will introduce some cooler air that should knock \nafternoon highs back down just below normal on Thursday. \nUnfortunately, that high pressure exerts itself yet again on \nFriday and beyond to push afternoon highs up into the triple \ndigits for our lower elevation valleys. Monsoonal moisture will \ndial back too, taking some of the flooding risks with it. \nAfternoon showers and thunderstorms will also lose some of their\ndynamic support. This will reduce widespread hydrological \nconcerns. However, isolated cases of heavy rain will still pose \na flood risk near saturated soils and sensitive burn scars.\n\nThis weekend high pressure arcs across the Rockies and the Great \nPlains. Weak monsoonal moisture will continue to trickle in, but \nwidespread upticks are not expected until perhaps early next week. \nTemperatures will trend 5 to 10 degrees above normal for much of the \nregion. Afternoon showers on the terrain will offer some relief to \nthe mountains and a few valleys. Models begin to hint at a better \nplume of moisture on Monday. This could increase storm coverage, but \nit's still a little premature to get committed. A tropical feature \nworking along the west coast of Mexico could send some better \nmoisture up our way mid week.",
